                     80386 CHECK ENCODER CALLING CONVENTION

OVERVIEW

This document describes the standard register based calling conventions used by
the MOORE/CAD check encoder for 80386.

IMPLEMENTATION

All parameters, and the return value, are passed via the stack. All objects on
the stack are rounded up in size to the nearest 32 bit word. This rounding
takes place in two different ways:

    Ordinals - If the object is signed, it is sign extended to be 32 bits. If
    the object is unsigned, it is zero extended to be 32 bits.

    Structures - The object occupies an even number of 32 bit words. The
    padding value at the end of the structure is undefined. The padding
    requirement only exists on the entire structure as passed. Any
    substructures occupy some number of bytes as per normal memory structures.

The order in which parameters are passed is right acending. That is, the
parameters will appear on the stack starting with the rightmost, proceeding to
the leftmost.
If a function return is present, it must exist as a single 32 bit "dummy"
parameter placed the stack before all the parameters. This will receive the
return result, and remain on the stack after the called function returns.
The format of a stack call frame is:

    ~              ~
    |              |
    |==============|
    | Function     |
    | Result       |
    |==============|
    | Param 1      |
    |==============|
    | Param 2      |
    |==============|
    ~              ~
    ~              ~
    |==============|
    | Param N      |
    |==============|
    | Return       |
    | Address      |
    |==============|
    |              |
    ~              ~

Where Param 1 is the leftmost parameter of the call, and Param N is the
rightmost parameter.

SYSTEM CALL THUNKS

By convention, Microsoft Windows 95 system calls are accessed by "thunks",
whose label consists of the system call label with "SC_" prepended:

    BeginPaint(hWnd, lpPaint);

becomes:

    SC_BeginPaint(hWnd, lpPaint);

This allows the call to be routed to a thunk. Microsoft system calls are
similar to the normal system call convention. The differences are:

    1. Being "C" oriented, Microsoft does not allow value parameters. but this
       just means that Pascal parameters are a superset of Microsoft
       parameters.

    2. Microsoft stacks parameters in reverse order (right to left) to allow
       variable argument calls. This means that the "C" header call order must
       be reversed in a Pascal list. This only means that the Pascal equivalent
       parameters be listed in the reverse order. Obviously, we cannot perform
       variable length argument calls, but there are no system calls that
       require this.

    3. Microsoft places the return parameter to be in the eax register on
       return.

To perform a thunk, then, all that is really required is that we place the
result returned from a microsoft function onto the stack before returning.
In addition, however, we also implement the call as an indirect call, as 
required by the Windows 95 loader. So a typical function thunk routine is:

    sc_mycall: call     [mycall]       ! execute system call
               pop      ebx            ! get return address
               push     eax            ! place return value
               jmp      [ebx]          ! return to program

And for a procedure thunk it would be:

    sc_mycall: call     [mycall]       ! execute system call
               ret                     ! exit

In this case, all that the thunk is doing is performing the indirect call.